4. Case Study
4.1. Scenario Overview
Case study description:  7 year retention example.
"To support the claim that immutability is essentially an operational constraint, the case study delves into a real-world cloud-
based audit logging pipeline incorporating append-only logs with immutable object storage under retention lock. The design of the
system followed a compliance audit that revealed inadequacies in the integrity and retention of logs. The management team
insisted that all security-related events, i.e., authentication logs, privileged actions, and infrastructure changes, should be stored in a
tamperproof way for at least seven years."

Choices for immutability across the board led to a deterministic outcome for the scope of damage.
" it decided how
the damage was going to spread and therefore, how it became very difficult to get better and fix the problems"

4.2. Incident / Operational Event
A simple change had a design error that led to incorrect accountability and attribution in logs.
"unnoticed edge case"
"the audit log
became a means to falsely record the administrative actions"

"the main constraint was that the wrong data had already been
written to the immutable storage under a retention lock. This meant that they were not allowed to delete, change, or overwrite the
affected files"

4.3. Response and Mitigation
common rollback and containment
"to a point of compensating records and annotation layers"
created a correction manifest
had to append more data to explain the incorrect data
needed to create instructions for future use to prevent mistakes related to the incorrect data


5. Results and Discussion
5.1. Key Findings
"security was more about having strong identity controls than the technology of
immutability itself"

"a trusted writer ... making faulty records."

"Therefore, identity management, least privilege, key rotation, and
detection mechanisms are, in fact, more important than immutability in determining a real security posture."

5.2. Discussion: Immutability ≠ Security
"Immutability does not ascertain that the data
is correct"

"One way to see it is that security goes beyond storage alone. It is the result of a combination of access control, monitoring,
detection, incident response capacity, and governance. Immutability can help make evidence last and also make some types of
alterations less tempting, but it is not a substitute for strong IAM, secure pipelines, and separation of duties. Actually, immutable
architectures can even heighten the need for these controls because errors and breaches are more difficult to be fixed."

6. Conclusion and Future Scope
6.1. Conclusion
"which is great for audits, forensics, and
accountability. However, the case study and subsequent analysis also reveal that the implementation of immutability brings about a
set of practical problems: on top of the fact that mistakes become very hard to fix, wrong data can be being there forever, and the
response to the security incidents frequently necessitates layered workarounds such as compensating records, re-indexing, or
rebuilding state from trusted checkpoints."

